ing variables\nstudent_name = "Jordan"\nmath_score = 88\nscience_score = 92\n\n# Using arithmetic operators to find the average\naverage_score = (math_score + science_score) / 2\n\n# Printing the result\nprint(student_name, "scored an average of:", average_score)\n```\n\n#### Summary\nMastering basic Python data types, variables, lists, and dictionaries establishes the core syntax required to interact with data analysis libraries.\n\n---\n\n### Module 2: Working with Pandas\n\n#### Introduction\nPandas is the foundational Python library used by data scientists to load, manipulate, and analyze structured tabular data quickly.\n\n#### Explanation\nPandas introduces two primary data structures:\n1. **Series**: A one-dimensional labeled array capable of holding any data type (similar to a single column in a spreadsheet).\n2. **DataFrame**: A two-dimensional, size-mutable tabular data structure with labeled rows and columns (similar to an entire spreadsheet or SQL table).\n\nTo work with real data, you typically read files (like CSVs) into a DataFrame, inspect its structure, and select specific subsets of data:\n- `df.head()`: Shows the first few rows of a DataFrame.\n- `df.info()`: Displays summary information, including column names, non-null counts, and data types.\n- `loc` and `iloc`: Methods used for indexing and selecting rows and columns. `loc` is label-based, while `iloc` is integer-position based.\n\n**Worked Example:**\nSuppose you have a CSV file named `sales.csv` and want to load and inspect it:\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Read a CSV file into a DataFrame\ndf = pd.read_csv("sales.csv")\n\n# View the first 5 rows\nprint(df.head())\n\n# Inspect the DataFrame structure (columns, data types, missing values)\nprint(df.info())\n\n# Select a specific column using label indexing\nrevenue_column = df[\'Revenue\']\n\n# Select the first row using integer-positional indexing (iloc)\nfirst_row = df.iloc[0]\n```\n\n#### Summary\nPandas DataFrames and Series provide powerful tabular structures that make loading, inspecting, and selecting subsets of data straightforward and efficient.\n\n---\n\n### Module 3: Data Cleaning and Preparation\n\n#### Introduction\nReal-world data is rarely clean; preparing and sanitizing your dataset ensures that your subsequent analysis is accurate and reliable.\n\n#### Explanation\nData hygiene involves identifying and resolving common data quality issues:\n1. **Missing Values**: Real datasets often contain blank entries (`NaN`). You can detect them and either remove rows/columns using `dropna()` or fill them in with substitute values (like a mean or placeholder) using `fillna()`.\n2. **Duplicate Rows**: Accidental duplicate entries can skew your statistics. You can detect duplicates and remove them using Pandas methods to keep your dataset unique.\n3. **Data Types and Column Names**: Sometimes numbers are stored as text (strings), or column names have awkward spaces. You can convert data types and rename columns to standardize your table for analysis.\n\n**Worked Example:**\nCleaning a messy DataFrame by filling missing values, dropping duplicates, and renaming columns:\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\n\ndata = {\'ID\': [1, 2, 2, 3], \'Score\': [85, None, None, 95], \'Name\': [\'Alice\', \'Bob\', \'Bob\', \'Charlie\']}\ndf = pd.DataFrame(data)\n\n# 1. Remove duplicate rows\ndf = df.drop_duplicates()\n\n# 2. Fill missing values in the \'Score\' column with the number 0\ndf[\'Score\'] = df[\'Score\'].fillna(0)\n\n# 3. Rename columns to ensure consistent capitalization\ndf = df.rename(columns={\'ID\': \'student_id\', \'Score\': \'test_score\'})\n\nprint(df)\n```\n\n#### Summary\nData cleaning techniques like handling missing values, removing duplicates, and standardizing columns turn messy inputs into analysis-ready datasets.\n\n---\n\n### Module 4: Basic Data Visualization\n\n#### Introduction\nData visualization translates numerical tables into visual charts, making it much easier to spot trends, outliers, and distributions at a glance.\n\n#### Explanation\nPython offers foundational plotting libraries like **Matplotlib** and **Seaborn** to create various types of charts:\n1. **Line Plots**: Ideal for showing trends over time or continuous sequences.\n2. **Scatter Plots**: Useful for examining the relationship between two numerical variables.\n3. **Bar Charts**: Great for comparing categorical values or counts across groups.\n4. **Histograms**: Perfect for inspecting the distribution and frequency of a single numerical variable.\n\n**Worked Example:**\nCreating a basic scatter plot and histogram using Matplotlib and Seaborn:\n```python\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport seaborn as sns\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Sample data\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    \'Hours_Studied\': [2, 3, 5, 7, 8, 10],\n    \'Exam_Score\': [50, 55, 70, 85, 90, 95]\n})\n\n# 1. Create a scatter plot to show relationship between studying and scores\nsns.scatterplot(data=df, x=\'Hours_Studied\', y=\'Exam_Score\')\nplt.title(\'Study Hours vs Exam Score\')\nplt.show()\n\n# 2. Create a histogram to show the distribution of exam scores\nplt.hist(df[\'Exam_Score\'], bins=3)\nplt.title(\'Distribution of Exam Scores\')\nplt.xlabel(\'Score\')\nplt.ylabel(\'Frequency\')\nplt.show()\n```\n\n#### Summary\nVisualizations using Matplotlib and Seaborn allow you to visually communicate distributions, comparisons, and relationships within your data.\n\n---\n\n### Module 5: Introductory Exploratory Analysis\n\n#### Introduction\nExploratory Data Analysis (EDA) is the crucial first step where you investigate a new dataset to uncover patterns, spot anomalies, and summarize core characteristics.\n\n#### Explanation\nWhen starting with a brand-new dataset, exploratory analysis combines summary statistics with visual plots to build an intuition for the data:\n1. **Summary Statistics**: Compute metrics such as mean, median, standard deviation, minimum, and maximum values to understand central tendencies and spreads.\n2. **Spotting Anomalies**: Identify unexpected outliers, extreme maximums, or impossible values (such as a negative age).\n3. **Synthesizing Insights**: Combine Pandas aggregation methods with visualization tools (like histograms and scatter plots) to form hypotheses and summarize the main properties of the data.\n\n**Worked Example:**\nPerforming basic exploratory analysis on a dataset of housing prices:\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport seaborn as sns\n\n# Sample housing dataset\nhousing = pd.DataFrame({\n    \'Area_SqFt\': [1200, 1500, 1800, 2400, 3000],\n    \'Price\': [200000, 250000, 300000, 450000, 600000]\n})\n\n# Compute summary statistics for numerical columns\nsummary_stats = housing.describe()\nprint(summary_stats)\n\n# Use a visualization to check for patterns and anomalies\nsns.scatterplot(data=housing, x=\'Area_SqFt\', y=\'Price\')\nplt.title(\'House Price vs. Square Footage\')\nplt.xlabel(\'Area (Sq Ft)\')\nplt.ylabel(\'Price ($)\')\nplt.show()\n```\n\n#### Summary\nExploratory data analysis blends summary statistics and visual inspection to reveal patterns, detect anomalies, and summarize the key characteristics of any new dataset.'}, artifact_delta={}, transfer_to_agent=None, escalate=None, requested_auth_configs={}, requested_tool_confirmations={}, compaction=None, end_of_agent=None, agent_state=None, rewind_before_invocation_id=None, route=None, render_ui_widgets=None, set_model_response=None) output=None node_info=NodeInfo(path='', output_for=None, message_as_output=None) long_running_tool_ids=None branch=None isolation_scope=None id='555c54bc-163d-4a46-a417-ebb141a97780' timestamp=1789112324.641007
