hon. The primary plotting function, `plt.plot()`, connects a series of data points across horizontal ($x$) and vertical ($y$) axes using straight lines. You can customize these visual outputs by modifying line properties—such as adding markers to emphasize specific data points, changing line styles and widths, and applying different colors. Because plotting functions build graphs in memory rather than displaying them automatically, you use a function like `plt.show()` to render the final image on your screen. In addition to line plots, you can create bar charts using `bar()`, pie charts using `pie()`, add background grids with `grid()`, or arrange multiple graphs in a grid using `subplot()`.\n\nFor example, to plot monthly sales data, you define your $x$ coordinates (months) and $y$ coordinates (sales totals), call `plt.plot(x, y, color='g', marker='s')` to draw a green line with square markers, and finish with `plt.show()` to display the chart.\n\n**Summary:** The `matplotlib.pyplot` library enables you to turn numbers into clear visual charts using functions like `plt.plot()` and `plt.show()`. By customizing markers, colors, and layouts, you can effectively present data insights to any audience."}, artifact_delta={}, transfer_to_agent=None, escalate=None, requested_auth_configs={}, requested_tool_confirmations={}, compaction=None, end_of_agent=None, agent_state=None, rewind_before_invocation_id=None, route=None, render_ui_widgets=None, set_model_response=None) output=None node_info=NodeInfo(path='', output_for=None, message_as_output=None) long_running_tool_ids=None branch=None isolation_scope=None id='f05287ed-a23c-4be5-ba7b-c9e12522a1f3' timestamp=1789114400.2293515
