DEBUG    | 2026-09-11 14:32:04 | course_creator:run_adk_course_generation:113 | Event from ContentWriterAgent: model_version='gemini-3.5-flash-lite' content=Content(
  parts=[
    Part(
      text="""### Introduction to Packages

When writing code, you often want to reuse existing code written by others rather than building everything from scratch. Packages allow developers to organize and share code efficiently.

A module is a single Python file containing code such as functions, classes, and variables. A package is a collection of these modules organized together in a directory structure. When one project or package relies on another external package to function correctly, that external package is known as a dependency. 

By utilizing packages, you can save time and leverage powerful tools built by the wider programming community.

---

### Using pip and PyPI

To find and download external Python packages, developers use the Python Package Index, commonly known as PyPI. PyPI is the official public repository and online catalog where open-source third-party Python packages are hosted and shared.

To download and manage packages from PyPI, you use `pip`, a package manager written in Python. From your command line or terminal, you can run commands to install or update packages. For example, to make sure your package manager is up to date, you can run:

`pip install --upgrade pip`

You can also use flags like `-U` to update existing packages to their latest versions.

---

### Installing and Importing Packages

Once you know which package you need, you can install it on your computer using terminal commands. For instance, to install popular data analysis or plotting tools, you can run:

`pip install numpy` or `pip install matplotlib`

Once a package is successfully installed, you can bring it into your Python script using an `import` statement. For example:

`import matplotlib.pyplot as plt`
`import numpy as np`

If the Python interpreter generates errors such as an `ImportError` or `ModuleNotFoundError`, it typically indicates that the package has not been installed correctly or is missing from your active environment.

---

### Working with Virtual Environments

*(Note: Insufficient source material was found in the uploaded document to cover this module in detail.)*

---

### Exploring Common Standard Library Packages

It is important to distinguish between third-party packages, which require explicit installation via `pip`, and Python's built-in Standard Library. Python comes pre-installed with a rich standard library containing many built-in modules for common tasks—such as math, random, and datetime—which can be imported and used immediately without running any installation commands."""
    ),
  ],
  role='model'
